I get the analogy — you want two different behaviors (change one field vs. wipe/replace the whole document) — but here's the direct answer to your actual question: your current code cannot do both, because it only has one function, and that function only does the "change one field" behavior.

To actually get both behaviors, you need two separate functions, not two routes pointing at the same function. Here's exactly what that looks like:

Function 1 — partial update (what you already have, keep this exactly as-is):

javascript
const UpDateQuestion = async(req,res)=>{
    const {id} = req.params;
    try{
        const updateQuestion = await Question.findByIdAndUpdate(
            id,
            req.body,
            { new: true, runValidators: true }
        );
        if(!updateQuestion){
            return res.status(404).json({ message:'this id or question does not exist' })
        }
        return res.status(200).json({ message:'Question updated Successfully', data: updateQuestion })
    } catch(error){
        return res.status(500).json({ message:'something went wrong', data: error.message })
    }
}
Function 2 — full replace (new function you'd need to write, for the "wipe the old document" case):

javascript
const ReplaceQuestion = async(req,res)=>{
    const {id} = req.params;
    try{
        const replaceQuestion = await Question.findOneAndReplace(
            { _id: id },
            req.body,
            { new: true, runValidators: true }
        );
        if(!replaceQuestion){
            return res.status(404).json({ message:'this id or question does not exist' })
        }
        return res.status(200).json({ message:'Question replaced Successfully', data: replaceQuestion })
    } catch(error){
        return res.status(500).json({ message:'something went wrong', data: error.message })
    }
}
Then your routes would point to two different functions, not the same one:

javascript
router.put('/replaceQuestion/:id', limiter, auth, isAdmin, ReplaceQuestion);   // full replace
router.patch('/updateQuestion/:id', limiter, auth, isAdmin, UpDateQuestion);   // partial update
The core answer to your question: yes, you can have both situations you described — but only if you write a second function using findOneAndReplace instead of findByIdAndUpdate. Right now, you only have the first function. If you don't actually need the "wipe and replace the whole document" feature anywhere in your app, there's no reason to build ReplaceQuestion — but if you do want that behavior available, this is exactly what needs to be added.

