Yes, exactly — you've got it precisely right. Let me confirm it back clearly, using the exact functions to lock it in:

findByIdAndUpdate (your UpDateQuestion function):

Finds the specific document by its id.
Looks at whatever fields are in req.body.
Only changes those specific fields — anything you didn't include in req.body stays exactly as it was before, completely untouched.
Example: if the question document has { topic: "Math", difficulty: "hard", question: "..." }, and you send { difficulty: "easy" }, only difficulty changes — topic and question remain exactly as they were.
findOneAndReplace (the ReplaceQuestion function):

Finds the specific document.
Takes whatever you send in req.body and treats it as the entire new version of that document.
Anything not included in req.body gets wiped out — not left alone, actually removed/reset.
Example: same starting document { topic: "Math", difficulty: "hard", question: "..." } — if you send { difficulty: "easy" } through this function, the result would become just { difficulty: "easy" } — topic and question would be gone, because you're telling MongoDB "this is the complete replacement, throw away anything not in this."
So yes — your summary is exactly correct: update = find it, change only the specific field(s) you send; replace = find it, throw out the old version entirely, and put this new one in its place. That's the real, functional difference between the two — not just a naming/method choice, but genuinely different database behavior.